      "type": "h1",
      "text": "How to Fix a Gap Between Teeth? Treatment Options Compared"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Home Blog 09 Feb, 2026",
      "links": [
        {
          "href": "https://drdantegonzales.com/index.php",
          "text": "Home"
        },
        {
          "href": "https://drdantegonzales.com/blog.php",
          "text": "Blog"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "img",
      "src": "../assets/img/blogs/b-1.jpeg",
      "alt": "",
      "title": null
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Ever look at your smile in a photo and think, “Should I close this space or leave it?” Many people ask that same question, because a gap between teeth can feel like a style choice for one person and a stress point for another. In most cases, a tooth gap is linked to many factors such as jaw size, the gum tissue between the teeth, or oral habits."
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "A gap between front teeth often shows up between the two upper front teeth, so it draws attention fast. Let’s break down what causes it, when it turns into a problem, and which treatment fits your goals."
    },
    {
      "type": "h2",
      "text": "What Causes a Gap Between Teeth?"
    },
    {
      "type": "img",
      "src": "../assets/img/blogs/b-2.jpg",
      "alt": "",
      "title": null
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "A tooth gap can show up anywhere in the mouth, yet people spot it most between the upper front teeth. A gap wider than 0.5 millimeters is called a diastema. Causes include:"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Typical sequence in many other orthodontic treatments too:"
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Missing teeth or tooth loss: nearby teeth shift, so other teeth move, and spacing grows."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Undersized teeth, often upper lateral incisors: the jaw has more room than the tooth shape fills."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Thumb sucking and thumb habits, plus tongue thrusting: the tongue and lip push teeth apart over time."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Gum disease and periodontal disease: With advanced gum disease, bone loss starts, the jawbone support drops, and loose teeth can drift."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Oversized labial frenum : the lip frenulum can grow large between the front teeth and push them apart, and also pull thin tissue at the gum line."
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Also, misalignment issues and crooked teeth can come from a jaw and teeth size mismatch. That mismatch can affect upper and lower teeth, including lower teeth in the lower jaw."
    },
    {
      "type": "h2",
      "text": "When Does a Gap Between Teeth Need Treatment?"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Some gaps stay as a cosmetic issue. Still, others cause oral health issues. So ask yourself:"
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Does food pack in the space near the gums and raise oral health risk?"
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Do you see gum line changes, bleeding, or signs of gum problems?"
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Did you lose a tooth, or do you suspect missing bone support?"
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Do you want teeth closer for bite balance across the upper and lower teeth?"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "If you see gum swelling, breath changes, or pain, treat gum disease first. Also, keep up with regular dental check-ups."
    },
    {
      "type": "h2",
      "text": "Common Treatment Options for Fixing a Gap Between Teeth"
    },
    {
      "type": "img",
      "src": "../assets/img/blogs/b-3.jpg",
      "alt": "",
      "title": null
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Treatment Options for Fixing a Gap Between Teeth"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "You can fix gaps with cosmetic dentistry or orthodontic treatment, but the right plan depends on why the space formed."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Dental bonding: A cosmetic dentist applies tooth colored resin to natural teeth to close small gaps. It suits front teeth when you want a fast change."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Porcelain veneers: Veneers cover small gaps and reshape the look of pearly whites. This option fits people who also want shape and color changes."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Braces: Braces move two teeth, then the other teeth, into better alignment. Traditional braces use metal or ceramic brackets and wires. Many patients need 18 months to 3 years."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Clear aligners: Clear aligners like Invisalign use removable trays for mild to moderate spacing. They often run 6 to 18 months. They do not work well for gaps larger than 6 mm and severe cases."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Frenectomy: A frenectomy is a minor oral surgery that removes an oversized labial frenum that pushes teeth apart. Many plans pair this with orthodontic treatment."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Dental implant or bridge: If you have missing teeth, a dental implant fills the space after tooth loss. Dental bridges use nearby teeth as anchors to hold a prosthetic tooth."
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "If you want to treat gapped teeth for long-term stability, keep retainers in the plan. Also, avoid oral habits like thumb sucking and tongue thrusting, since they can reopen spaces."
    },
    {
      "type": "h2",
      "text": "Gap Between Teeth Treatment Options"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Different treatment options work for different gap sizes, causes, and timelines. Use this quick comparison to see which choice fits your mouth, goals, and budget."
    },
